      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-315194",
      ":question/problem": "The specific heat capacity of a substance is defined as the amount of energy, in joules $(J)$, required to raise the temperature of $1$ gram $(g)$ of the substance by $1$ degree Celsius $(^{\u2218}C)$. The specific heat capacity of copper is approximately $0.39\\frac{J}{g \\cdot ^{\u2218}C}$. Approximately how much energy, in joules, is required to raise the temperature of $1$ gram of copper from $20^{\u2218}C$ to $80^{\u2218}C$?"
